WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:08.040
Speaker 1: We now have reached a horrible&nbsp;
milestone in the United States where guns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:16.020
are the leading cause of child deaths for those&nbsp;
under 18. The New York Times had a very good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:21.540
piece on this a couple of days ago. And if you&nbsp;
look at leading causes of death for children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:28.800
ages one through 18, if you're looking on the&nbsp;
screen, you see this green line, which is guns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:39.000
which has now surpassed motor vehicle crashes at&nbsp;
19% of deaths for children age 1 to 18. It is guns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:47.760
followed by motor vehicle crashes, cancer drug&nbsp;
overdoses, which has increased and suffocation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:56.520
has decreased, putting it into fifth position&nbsp;
into fifth position this century. Okay. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:03.000
the year 2000, as you can see on the screen, if&nbsp;
you're looking, guns have risen as the cause of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:14.760
death from 10% of kids age one to 18 to 19%.&nbsp;
Nearly five in 100,000 die each year. Kids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.120
These are stunning numbers. Most&nbsp;
of the deaths are homicides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:31.560
Many of the victims are black and mostly boys.&nbsp;
There are also suicides. The suicides are mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:38.520
white and mostly boys. There are relatively few&nbsp;
of what would be called accidental deaths. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.060
take issue with the term accidental. Typically&nbsp;
you're talking about negligence, actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:48.000
but very few are what are often called accidental&nbsp;
deaths. We hear about, oh, a dad was cleaning his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.200
gun and shot his daughter dead. We do see those&nbsp;
stories, but those are actually relatively few.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:58.440
This is a uniquely American problem. And&nbsp;
in order to start thinking about solutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:04.500
we have to really understand the problem. This&nbsp;
is not a global epidemic. If you look globally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:12.480
the most common causes of death for children are&nbsp;
infectious diseases 45% disproportionately in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:22.860
developing world, 15% pneumonia and respiratory&nbsp;
disease, 12% preterm birth in neonatal disorders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:32.280
10% diarrheal categorized disease, 9%&nbsp;
congenital defects. Now, as a math issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.660
it is true. I know people are going to write to&nbsp;
me and say this, so I will be very upfront with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:44.040
you. If you reduce one thing simply by comparison,&nbsp;
another thing is going to go up. So in the U.S.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:50.940
we have certainly reduced some of these elements&nbsp;
as causes of death. Right. Diarrheal disease,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.220
death in the United States, in kids is a lot rarer&nbsp;
than in other parts of the country. So if you drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:03.000
that, then other stuff will go up like gun deaths.&nbsp;
But even in the rest of the developed world where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.420
there isn't much diarrheal child death, you do&nbsp;
not see gun death numbers like we see in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:14.820
United States. So what are the differences&nbsp;
between the U.S. and other countries? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:19.560
we have one of the highest rates of gun ownership.&nbsp;
Is it that alone? No, but that's a factor. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:24.840
the highest rates of gun ownership. So there are&nbsp;
guns around kids. We have a lack of strict gun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:33.060
control laws in most states. We have a culture&nbsp;
that glorifies violence and exposes even kids. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:38.400
mean, you know, we're talking 14, 15, 16, 17 year&nbsp;
olds completely exposed and affected by that very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:44.520
culture that glorifies violence, sees guns as&nbsp;
a way to solve problems. High rate of poverty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:50.340
high rate of income inequality, both of which have&nbsp;
been studied in connection to guns as a public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:55.800
health issue and lack of access to mental health&nbsp;
resources. Now, of course, I have no interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.220
in ignoring any of these aspects. The right&nbsp;
loves to say it's just mental health. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.880
they don't want to fund mental health.&nbsp;
Mental health is certainly a factor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:12.600
but availability of guns, widespread nature of&nbsp;
guns, that's a factor as well. There are clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:19.380
steps we can take. I've outlined so many of them&nbsp;
so many times before. No one step will solve this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:24.480
problem. No one step will solve the problem. And&nbsp;
we always hear after a shooting, well, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:30.360
your idea of limit limiting clip capacity&nbsp;
wouldn't have prevented this particular shooting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.780
I don't know anyone who claims any one thing&nbsp;
when you have a problem that's based on 6 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:41.940
8 things. There's no one thing that will&nbsp;
solve that problem. But we have to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:47.160
on all of these things. And quite frankly,&nbsp;
the numbers speak for themselves unique to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:53.040
the United States. If you want to let me put it&nbsp;
this way. If you want to find other countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:59.340
with a gun violence problem affecting kids to&nbsp;
the degree that we have in the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.760
I'm not going to name the countries. You will&nbsp;
find countries that none of these right wingers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:12.000
would say are in any way equivalent to the&nbsp;
United States. So take a look. We're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.360
to link to the study in The New York Times.&nbsp;
We're going to continue talking about all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.880
it. But we have plenty of ideas. But we have a&nbsp;
right wing that is unwilling to actually try any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:26.820
